{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1390,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "E=mc^2, the most famous equation in the world,\ndescribes the fact that anything with mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6899,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possesses a huge amount of energy, in principle\n– like, a 5kg cat has enough energy in its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13350,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass to power the entire country of Norway\nfor a year – if only the energy could somehow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18280,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be fully extracted from the cat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20699,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that efficiently extracting\nenergy from mass is a very hard thing to do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25820,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anti-matter is, of course, the most efficient\nway of extracting energy from mass since,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29900,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you collide a cat with a cat made of anti-matter,\n100% of the mass of the cat and anti-cat will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34660,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be converted into energy (powering Norway\nfor 2 years)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38460,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the universe has almost no naturally-occurring\nanti-matter, so it’s not a practical choice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42829,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for generating energy, since you’d first\nhave to use a lot of energy to make a large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47059,
    "dDurationMs": 1571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48630,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since we can't use antimatter, there are basically\nthree options left to us: chemical reactions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54190,
    "dDurationMs": 2509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear reactions, and gravitational reactions\n- aka stuff getting pulled together by gravity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56699,
    "dDurationMs": 2961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like matter falling into black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59660,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chemical reactions, for example, are so bad\nat extracting energy from mass that we don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63480,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even think about what they’re doing as converting\nmass to energy (even though it is)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68110,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As an illustration, reacting a balloon of\nhydrogen and oxygen gases creates a nice big"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73110,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosion, but the end-products of the reaction\nonly weigh half a nanogram less than the initial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78140,
    "dDurationMs": 8900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactants , which amounts to a measly 0.00000001%\nefficiency of converting mass into energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that rate, you’d need ten billion cats\nto power Norway for a year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91840,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclear reactions are a lot more efficient,\nbut still pretty bad on an absolute scale:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97110,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "splitting uranium-235 into krypton and barium\nconverts only about 0.08% of the uranium’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102930,
    "dDurationMs": 5789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass into energy, and fusing hydrogen into\nhelium like in the sun converts about 0.7%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108719,
    "dDurationMs": 2121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the hydrogen’s mass into energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110840,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that rate you’d need 150 cats to power\nNorway for a year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114880,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This where black holes come in – they’re\nabout as good as it gets in our universe for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118880,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extracting energy from mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121109,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which may sound weird, because, as you’ve\nprobably heard, nothing can escape black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125319,
    "dDurationMs": 1831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– once inside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127150,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the efficiency of black holes comes from\nwhat stuff does while falling towards them,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131260,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before passing the no-turning-back point of\nthe event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135480,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anything that falls in a gravitational field\nspeeds up, gaining kinetic energy, and if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139870,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it then crashes into something it can convert\nthat kinetic energy into heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144120,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That heat can then radiate away as infrared\nradiation, slightly decreasing the mass of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148870,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150010,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For planets and stars, this conversion of\nmass into energy is pretty pathetic: an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154620,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "falling to the surface of the earth releases\nonly about one billionth of its mass as energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159440,
    "dDurationMs": 8100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s basically as bad as a chemical reaction!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167540,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But black holes have something special going\nfor them: they are stupendouslysmall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172019,
    "dDurationMs": 5141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A black hole with the mass of the earth would\nbe about 2 cm across, providing way farther"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177160,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for an object to fall – and since gravity\ngets stronger and stronger the closer you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181010,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are to an object, objects falling into black\nholes get accelerated to ridiculous speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Specifically, an object falling all the way\nto the event horizon of a black hole will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190840,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have kinetic energy equivalent to converting\nroughly half of its half of its E=mc2 mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195730,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196730,
    "dDurationMs": 3729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, if the object continues to fall into\nthe black hole, all of that energy will be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200459,
    "dDurationMs": 1731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuck inside the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202190,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The way to actually convert mass into energy\nthat goes out into the universe is to have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206240,
    "dDurationMs": 5219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the object slowly spiral into the black hole,\ncrashing into other stuff, heating up, radiating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211459,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that energy away thereby losing mass and speed,\nslowing down more, spiraling to a yet lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216760,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit, and so on, all the way down to the\ninnermost possible orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220520,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is exactly what accretion disks around\nblack holes do!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224220,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how good are they at converting mass to\nenergy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226520,
    "dDurationMs": 5139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, for a non-rotating black hole, the innermost\npossible circular orbit is actually 3 times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231659,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "farther out than the event horizon, and in\norder to spiral in to that point an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235370,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to convert around 6% of its mass into\nenergy radiated away to the outside universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240560,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After that point if it loses any more energy\nit’ll plunge down into the black hole, after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244330,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which no more energy can be extracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246310,
    "dDurationMs": 6739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But at this 6% rate, you’d only need to\nthrow 17 cats into a black hole to power Norway"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253049,
    "dDurationMs": 1071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254120,
    "dDurationMs": 7641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to the 0.00000001% efficiency of\nchemical reactions and the 0.7% efficiency"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261761,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nuclear reactions, 6% for a non-rotating\nblack hole may seem pretty good."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266670,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But rotating black holes are even better,\nbecause of how they bend spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270350,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They literally “drag” things orbiting\nthem in the direction of their rotation, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275421,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means the innermost possible orbit can be\nmuch closer to the black hole (as long as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278970,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re rotating along with the black hole)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279970,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The details depend on how fast the black hole\nis rotating, but for a very quickly rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283160,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole the innermost possible orbit coincides\nwith the event horizon!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289290,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the event horizon itself is half as big\nas for a non-rotating black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293390,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined together, this means that matter\nfalling into rotating black holes can convert"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297730,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as much as 42% of its mass into energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300760,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or equivalently, you’d only need 2 and a\nhalf inspiralling cats to power Norway for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305600,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if you really want to convert the mass\nof an object into energy, don’t bother with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312200,
    "dDurationMs": 5032,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chemical reactions, or nuclear fission, or\nnuclear fusion: throw it into a rapidly rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317232,
    "dDurationMs": 3298,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320530,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’re wondering how I calculated the\nefficiencies of converting mass to energy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324420,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can just divide the energy any reaction\nreleases by the mass energy of the things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329530,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "involved – for example, when radium radioactively\ndecays into radon and helium it releases 6.6"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335370,
    "dDurationMs": 5871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MeV of energy, and the mass energy of a single\nneutron or proton is about 940MeV, so I’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341241,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leave it to you to figure out how efficient\nalpha decay is at converting mass to energy!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345470,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or you can learn more about nuclear fission\nand fusion by finishing this quiz on Brilliant.org,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350800,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is this video’s sponsor and is full\nof interactive quizzes and mini courses on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354390,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics and math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355550,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you really want to understand physics deeply,\nyou have to work through calculations and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359100,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solve problems yourself, and Brilliant offers\nan interactive online way to do just that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363680,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can check out their course on black holes\nfor free using the link in the description,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367530,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if you decide to sign up for premium access\nto all of their courses, you can get 20% off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371431,
    "dDurationMs": 1189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by going to Brilliant.org/minutephysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372620,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, that’s Brilliant.org/minutephysics\nwhich lets Brilliant know you came from here."
    } ]
  } ]
}
